<question type="description"><name><text>Module 3 · Lesson 12 · Section 5: The Numbers 1-10</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 5: The Numbers 1-10</h3>
<p>Semitic numbers from 3 to 10 famously exhibit “gender
polarity”—masculine nouns take the numeral form ending in <em>-ā</em>
(which looks morphologically feminine), while feminine nouns take the
short numeral form without <em>-ā</em>. For 1 and 2, the numeral agrees
directly in gender with the noun.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th style="text-align: left;">Number</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">With Masc. Nouns</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">With Fem. Nouns</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>1</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܚܕ (ḥad)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܚܕܐ (ḥdā)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">(One)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>2</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܬܪܝܢ (trēn)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܬܪܬܝܢ (tartēn)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">(Two)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>3</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܬܠܬܐ (tlātā)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܬܠܬ (tlāt)</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">(Three)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
