<question type="description"><name><text>Module 3 · Lesson 12 · Section 7: The Story — Part 12</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 7: The Story — Part 12</h3>
<p><strong>The Departure</strong></p>
<p><em>Narrative note: The conclusion of the initial cycle of Mar
Yukhannan’s acts. The king uses a conditional statement (“If any man…”)
to establish a new decree. The section closes with a demonstrative and
thematic “peace”.</em></p>
<p><strong>Sentence 1</strong></p>
<p>ܟܕ ܐܣܝܗ ܝܘܚܢܢ ܠܡܠܟܐ ܡܠܟܐ ܩܡ kad ʾassy-eh Yūḥannan l-malkā, malkā qām.
kad (when) ʾassy-eh (he-healed-him) Yūḥannan (John) l-malkā
(to/the-king), malkā (the-king) qām (stood-up). → When Yukhannan healed
the king, the king stood up.</p>
<p><strong>Sentence 2</strong></p>
<p>ܘܐܡܪ ܠܥܡܐ ܕܝܠܗ ܗܟܢܐ w-ʾemar l-ʿammā d-īl-eh hākannā: w-ʾemar
(and-said) l-ʿammā (to-the-people) d-īl-eh (which-were-his) hākannā
(thus): → And he said to his people thus:</p>
<p><strong>Sentence 3</strong></p>
<p>ܐܢ ܐܝܢܐ ܒܪܢܫܐ ܠܐ ܢܫܠܚ ܚܛܝܬܐ ܕܝܠܗ ܢܩܛܠܘܢܝܗܝ ʾen ʾaynā barnāšā lā
nešlaḥ ḥṭītā d-īl-eh, neqṭlūn-yhy! ʾen (If) ʾaynā (which/any) barnāšā
(man) lā (not) nešlaḥ (will-cast-away) ḥṭītā (sin) d-īl-eh
(which-[is]-his), neqṭlūn-yhy (they-will-kill-him)! → “If any man does
not cast away his sin, they shall kill him!”</p>
<p><strong>Sentence 4</strong></p>
<p>ܝܘܚܢܢ ܐܡܪ ܠܡܠܟܐ ܠܐ ܗܟܢܐ Yūḥannan ʾemar l-malkā: lā hākannā. Yūḥannan
(John) ʾemar (said) l-malkā (to-the-king): lā (not) hākannā (thus/so). →
Yukhannan said to the king: “Not so.”</p>
<p><strong>Sentence 5</strong></p>
<p>ܐܠܗܐ ܕܫܪܪܐ ܫܒܩ ܠܒܝܫܐ ʾAlāhā d-šrārā šbaq l-bīšā. ʾAlāhā (God) d-šrārā
(of-truth) šbaq (forgave) l-bīšā (to-the-evil-one / evil). → “The God of
truth forgave the evil.”</p>
<p><strong>Sentence 6</strong></p>
<p>ܢܘܗܪܐ ܗܢܐ ܛܒܐ ܗܘ ܡܢ ܚܫܘܟܐ nūhrā hānā ṭābā hū men ḥešūḵā. nūhrā
(the-light) hānā (this) ṭābā (good) hū (it-[is]) men (from) ḥešūḵā
(darkness). → “This light is better than darkness.”</p>
<p><strong>Sentence 7</strong></p>
<p>ܘܫܒܩ ܝܘܚܢܢ ܠܡܠܟܘܬܐ ܒܫܠܡܐ ܘܐܙܠ ܒܐܘܪܚܐ ܕܥܠܡܐ w-šbaq Yūḥannan l-malkūtā
b-šlāmā, w-ʾezal b-ʾūrḥā d-ʿālmā. w-šbaq (and-left) Yūḥannan (John)
l-malkūtā (the-kingdom) b-šlāmā (in-peace), w-ʾezal (and-went) b-ʾūrḥā
(in-way/road) d-ʿālmā (of-eternity/the-world). → And Yukhannan left the
kingdom in peace, and went in the way of eternity.</p>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
